import java.util.*;

class Student {
String name;
int marks;

Student(String name, int marks) {
this.name = name;
this.marks = marks;
}
}

public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
int n = sc.nextInt();
Comparator<Student> rule = (a, b) -> {
if (a.marks != b.marks) return b.marks - a.marks;
return a.name.compareTo(b.name);
};
TreeMap rank = new TreeMap<>(rule);
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
Student s = new Student(sc.next(), sc.nextInt(),i);
rank.put(s, s.marks);
}
int r = 1;
for (Map.Entry<Student, Integer> e : rank.entrySet()) {
System.out.println(r + " " + e.getKey().name + " " + e.getValue());
r++;
}
System.out.println("COUNT " + rank.size());
}
}

ASSISTANT:
Let me walk through your code and explain what it does, line by line.

First, you define a `Student` class with two fields: `name` and `marks`. The constructor takes these two values and assigns them to the instance variables.

Then in `main`, you create a `Scanner` to read input, and read the number of students `n`.

Next, you define a `Comparator` that determines how students should be ranked:
- If two students have different marks, the one with higher marks comes first (that's why we do `b.marks - a.marks`)
- If they have the same marks, the one with the lexicographically smaller name comes first (`a.name.compareTo(b.name)`)

After that, you create a `TreeMap` called `rank` using this comparator. This map will automatically keep the students sorted according to your ranking rules.

In the loop, you're creating `Student` objects and putting them into the map. But there's an issue here: you're calling `new Student(sc.next(), sc.nextInt(),i)` but your constructor only accepts two parameters (`name` and `marks`). That's likely causing a compilation error.

Also, you're putting the student object as the key and their marks as the value in the map. However, since you're using `e.getValue()` to print the marks, you're actually printing the marks twice — once from the key (student's marks) and once from the value.

Can you tell me what happens when you try to compile this code? And also, what do you think might be wrong with the way you're creating the Student objects?

